POPULATE () :

What is populate?
.> Populate is a Mongoose function that automatically fills referenced ObjectIds with actual documents using separate DB lookups.


What .populate() does ? :

consider we have something like this :
------------------------------------------- >
users :
{
  _id: "u1",
  name: "Sachin"
}
posts :
{
  _id: "p1",
  title: "My Post",
  userId: "u1"   // ONLY stores the user id, not the full user
}
after using the populate:
{
  _id: "p1",
  title: "My Post",
  userId: {
    _id: "u1",
    name: "Sachin"
  }
}


.> Looks in the other collection (users)
Finds the document for that id
And replaces the id with the full user object


When to use it:
-------------------- >
Small/medium datasets
One-to-one or simple one-to-many
For convenience, developer productivity
When you need full referenced document in the API response


Synatax:
----------- >
1) Post.find().populate("user");
2) Post.find().populate("user", "name email");
3) Post.find().populate({
  path: "user",
  select: "name"
});
4) Post.find()
  .populate("user")
  .populate("comments");

Mongoose is a library for Node.js that makes MongoDB easier by giving features like schemas, validation, and population.

different be the populate() and lookup :
6. When to use .populate() vs $lookup
✔ Use .populate() when:
You want simple API
You use Mongoose
You want quick JOIN-like behavior
You want readable code

✔ Use $lookup when:
You need performance optimization
You use raw MongoDB
You need complex joins or conditions
You want server-side processing




